  {
    "text": "Yes. Fluconazole is a moderate inhibitor of CYP2C9, and warfarin's more potent enantiomer (S-warfarin) is cleared primarily via CYP2C9 metabolism. Inhibiting CYP2C9 reduces S-warfarin clearance, raising plasma levels and augmenting anticoagulant effect (elevated INR, increased bleeding risk). This interaction is well documented clinically and may require INR monitoring and warfarin dose adjustment. Note: the provided card covers only fluconazole; warfarin's CYP2C9 dependence is from standard pharmacologic knowledge, not the supplied text.\n\nMECHANISM: Fluconazole inhibits CYP2C9, decreasing S-warfarin metabolism and increasing warfarin exposure\nFINAL ANSWER: YES",
